The Three Conditional Types
Type 1 — Real/Possible condition (about future)

If + Simple Present, ... Simple Future

If he comes, I will go.

Used for things that are likely or possible to happen.
"If" clause → simple present ("comes")
Main clause → will + base verb ("will go")

Type 2 — Unreal/Imaginary condition (about present/future)

If + Simple Past, ... would + base verb

If he came, I would go.
If I had enough money,I would buy a car.(Here had doesnt not represent past perfect, it acts as a simple past or did ,so would+base form will be used)

Used for hypothetical situations — imagining something that isn't currently true.
"If" clause → simple past ("came") — note: this isn't real past tense meaning, it's just the form used for unreality.
Main clause → would + base verb ("would go")


Type 3 — Unreal condition about the past (didn't happen)

If + Past Perfect, ... would have + past participle

If he had come, I would have gone.
If I had had enough money,I would have bought a car.(Here 2 hads are used,1st is auxilary , and 2nd is main(like past participle of have, had+v3(v3 of have is had)).

Used for imagining a different outcome to something that already happened (and can't be changed now).
"If" clause → past perfect ("had come")
Main clause → would have + past participle ("would have gone")
